// @kama/postgres — the PostgreSQL client for kama. It speaks the v3 wire protocol directly, in kama, over
// std::net; there is no libpq and nothing to install. TLS comes from @kama/tls.
//
// The shape of the package, as it fills in:
//   postgres            Config and Environment (resolved as libpq resolves them), Connection, Rows and typed
//                       access (`column::<T>`, `columnOpt::<T>`, `rowAs::<T>`), PgError and ServerError,
//                       NoticeHandler, Transport; later the `pg"…"` tag
//   postgres::conninfo  connection strings, the password file and the service file, as libpq reads them
//   postgres::protocol  the v3 wire protocol as data, for a proxy, a pooler or a test harness
//   postgres::sqlstate  every SQLSTATE, as packed integer constants
//   postgres::types     the value types PostgreSQL has and kama's std does not (Numeric, Interval, Inet, …)
//   postgres::pool      a connection pool shared by isolates
//
// The rules every module holds:
//   - Nothing from the server is trusted: every length is bounded before it is used, every text field is
//     validated as UTF-8, and a malformed message is `PgError::Protocol`, never a panic.
//   - A data error is a `Result`; a contract violation (a column index out of range) panics, exactly
//     like an index out of range on a collection.
//   - Blocking calls over a non-blocking socket, each step bounded by a deadline (libpq's design). A connection
//     belongs to one isolate at a time and moves between them.
//   - Every libpq setting is honoured, or refused with a clear error; none is silently ignored. Messages are
//     libpq's, word for word, wherever libpq has one.
